Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below are example Snowflake SQL queries (from exploratory to increasingly complex) that help you step‑through all of the columns that matter to the question  
“Which repository with an approved license in ​licenses.md​ had the highest combined total of forks, issues, and watches in April 2022?”

(Every identifier is double‑quoted; each query ends with LIMIT 20 so you can preview results safely.)

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at the LICENSES table to understand its shape
SELECT *
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: See the different license strings that appear
SELECT DISTINCT "license"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: List repositories that actually have a (non‑NULL) license string
SELECT DISTINCT "repo_name"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES"
WHERE "license" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Preview one month of raw events (April 2022) to learn the column layout
SELECT *
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
WHERE "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000   -- April 1 2022 00:00:00 µs  → April 30 23:59:59 µs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: What kinds of event "type" strings exist?
SELECT DISTINCT "type"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Sample just the three event types we care about in April 2022
SELECT "type", "repo", "id", "created_at"
FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
WHERE "type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
  AND "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Count April 2022 ForkEvents per repository
SELECT
       "repo": "name"::STRING          AS "repo_name",
       COUNT(*)                        AS "fork_events"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
WHERE  "type" = 'ForkEvent'
  AND  "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP  BY 1
ORDER  BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Gather IssuesEvent counts for the same window
SELECT
       "repo": "name"::STRING          AS "repo_name",
       COUNT(*)                        AS "issues_events"
FROM   "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
WHERE  "type" = 'IssuesEvent'
  AND  "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000
GROUP  BY 1
ORDER  BY 2 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Build a single April‑2022 table with all three event counts per repo
WITH apr22 AS (
    SELECT
        "repo": "name"::STRING        AS "repo_name",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'ForkEvent'   THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "forks",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'IssuesEvent' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "issues",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'WatchEvent'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "watches"
    FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
    WHERE "type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
      AND "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY 1
)
SELECT *
FROM apr22
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Join those April‑2022 counts to LICENSES,
--                 keep only repos whose license is *approved* in licenses.md,
--                 and rank by combined total (forks + issues + watches)
WITH apr22 AS (
    SELECT
        "repo": "name"::STRING                        AS "repo_name",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'ForkEvent'   THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "forks",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'IssuesEvent' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "issues",
        SUM( CASE WHEN "type" = 'WatchEvent'  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END ) AS "watches"
    FROM "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."YEAR"."_2022"
    WHERE "type" IN ('ForkEvent','IssuesEvent','WatchEvent')
      AND "created_at" BETWEEN 1648771200000000 AND 1651363199000000
    GROUP BY 1
)
SELECT
       a."repo_name",
       a."forks",
       a."issues",
       a."watches",
       (a."forks" + a."issues" + a."watches")      AS "total_activity",
       l."license"
FROM       apr22                                         a
INNER JOIN "GITHUB_REPOS_DATE"."GITHUB_REPOS"."LICENSES" l
        ON a."repo_name" = l."repo_name"
WHERE      l."license" ILIKE ANY ( ARRAY[
             '%apache%', '%mit%', '%gpl%', '%bsd%', '%mpl%', '%epl%'   -- <- add/adjust approved patterns from licenses.md
           ] )
ORDER BY    "total_activity" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

The final query (10) gives you the candidate list, sorted by the combined total of forks + issues + watches for April 2022, restricted to repositories whose license appears in the approved list held in licenses.md.
